      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "File your claim immediately by contacting your insurance agent with your policy number. Document all damage with photos and take temporary steps\u2014like covering holes with a tarp\u2014to prevent further interior destruction. Avoid hiring contractors for permanent repairs until an adjuster has evaluated the damage.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Navigating a roof insurance claim in Utah involves several specific steps and precautions:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Review Your Policy & Timeline: Check your policy for your specific deductible (typically ranging from $1,000 to $2,500 or a percentage of the home's value) and time limits. Many insurers require claims to be filed within 30 to 90 days of a damaging event.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3,
              4,
              7,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Beware of \"Storm Chasers\": Utah frequently experiences severe weather, which attracts transient, out-of-town roofing crews. Research and select a reputable local roofing contractor for a preliminary inspection to help assess if you have enough damage to warrant a claim.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "File the Claim: Call your insurer's claims department. Be prepared to provide the date the damage occurred and the suspected cause (e.g., wind, hail, or a fallen tree).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              9,
              10,
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Meet the Adjuster: Coordinate a time for your insurance company\u2019s adjuster to inspect your roof. Having your trusted roofing contractor present during this inspection can help ensure no hidden damage is missed."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Keep Receipts: Document and save all receipts for emergency repairs (such as tarps or plywood) as these may be reimbursable."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you feel your claim is being unfairly delayed or denied, you can file an official grievance or check your insurer's standing through the\nUtah Insurance Department.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "Utah Insurance Department",
            "link": "https://insurance.utah.gov/"
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          6,
          11,
          12
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you tell me the cause of the damage (e.g., hail, windstorm, or age) and what your homeowner's deductible is, I can help you weigh the pros and cons of filing a claim."
      }
    ],
